-- title:
--   Descended abelian quotients kill the Hopf cokernel of ψᵥ
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and a modulus $M$ with $p \mid M$ and $p^2 \nmid M$, a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ containing every unit that becomes $1$ in $(\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^\times$ (hypothesis `hHp`), and a valuation subring $Pl$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` lying over $p$ in the sense that $p$ belongs to the non-units of $Pl$ (`hPl`), whose residue field is of characteristic $p$ and algebraically closed. It is assumed that $j$, as a $q$-expansion, lies in the function field $\mathrm{qExpFunctionFieldC}\ \mathbb{Q}\ \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ (`hj`). The geometric frame consists of: a model $\mathfrak{X}$ of type [`ModularCurve.XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj`](def/ModularCurve_XHDRModelAtP.html#L81) for the two relevant levels; a level datum $\Lambda$ (a scheme $\Lambda.X$ over `base p` with relative group law $\Lambda.L$ and the two point parametrisations of the structure `LevelData`); and an object $O$ of type [`ModularCurve.JHNeronObjectAtP p M H hpM Pl hPl Λ`](def/ModularCurve_JHNeronObjectAtP.html#L53), i.e. a smooth separated surjective group scheme $O.G \to$ `base p` with relative group law $O.L$, parametrisation of $J_H = \mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the geometric function field of $X_H$, Hecke endomorphisms, and the toric data (toric rank $O.\mathrm{toricRank}$, subgroups $O.\mathrm{toricPts}$ and $O.\mathrm{finPts}$ of $J_H(M,H)$, the latter generated by the $m$-torsion classes whose section extends over $Pl$).
--
--   The representability hypotheses `hrep` and `hrepΛ` assert that the relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ designations built from $(O.G, O.g)$, respectively from $(\Lambda.X, \Lambda.f)$, together with their unit sections, represent the sub-Picard condition `algEquivZeroCut` (fibrewise algebraically trivial line bundles) on the two-chart integral models `toBase p (ΓM M H) hj` and `toBase p (ΓN p M H hpM) hj`, the second rigidified along $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\inf}$ composed with $\mathfrak{X}.\pi$.
--
--   A coefficient ring is fixed: $Rh$ is a henselian local domain which is a discrete valuation ring, equipped with an $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra structure with faithful scalar action, such that the image of $Rh$ lies in $Pl$ (`hRA`) and the maximal ideal of $Rh$ consists exactly of the elements of valuation $< 1$ (`hRloc`), together with an algebra map $Rh \to \mathbb{Z}/p$ whose kernel is described by the same valuation condition (`hres`). Auxiliary combinatorial data are a set $S$ of naturals and a unit $d \in (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ whose image in $\mathbb{Z}/(M/p)$ equals $p$ (`hd`).
--
--   The finite-part witness consists of a $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{G}$ over $Rh$ of height $h$ (a tower of finite free cocommutative Hopf algebras $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\ v$ with surjective transitions, $\operatorname{rank} = p^{vh}$ and kernel of the transition the $p^v$-torsion ideal), an additive map $\Delta$ from $\mathcal{G}$-points over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ to $J_H$, and a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-linear map $e$ of Tate modules (sequences $(x_n)$ with $p^n x_n = 0$ and $p x_{n+1} = x_n$). Its hypotheses are: injectivity of $\Delta$ (`hΔinj`); identification of $O.\mathrm{finPts}(p^v)$ with the image of the level-$v$ points under $\Delta$ (`hΔlev`); Galois equivariance of $\Delta$ for compatible pairs $(\tau, \tau')$ of automorphisms over $\mathbb{Q}$ and over $Rh$ (`hΔgal`); Hecke compatibility, namely that for every set $S$ of naturals and every generator $g$ of [`CohCarrier.Gen M S`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L13) there is a levelwise system $\varphi$ of bialgebra endomorphisms of $\mathcal{G}$, compatible with transitions, inducing `genOpH M H S g` on $J_H$ through $\Delta$ (`hΔhecke`); the coordinatewise formula $e(x)_n = \Delta(x_n)$ (`he`); injectivity of $e$ (`heinj`); the description of its range as the Tate vectors with $n$-th entry in $O.\mathrm{finPts}(p^n)$ (`herange`); Galois equivariance of $e$ for the Tate module representations (`hegal`); saturation of the range under division by $p$ (`hsat`); freeness of the cokernel, $\mathrm{coker}(e) \cong \mathbb{Z}_p^{O.\mathrm{toricRank}}$ (`hcoker`); and the inclusion of $O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p^v)$ in the image of the level-$v$ points (`htor`).
--
--   The toric complement consists of a $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{B}$ over $Rh$ of height $hB$ and a levelwise system $\psi$ of bialgebra maps $\mathcal{B}.\mathrm{level}\ v \to \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\ v$, with numerical hypotheses $h = O.\mathrm{toricRank} + hB$ and $hB = 2h'$, compatibility of $\psi$ with the transitions (`hψt`), the kernel description `hψker` (a level-$v$ point of $\mathcal{G}$ restricts trivially along $\psi_v$ precisely when its $\Delta$-image lies in $O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p^v)$), surjectivity of $\psi$ on $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points (`hψsurj`), a reduction criterion `hψred` (if the $\psi_v$-restriction of a point is congruent to the counit modulo the maximal ideal of $Pl$, so is the point itself) and the period hypothesis `hperiod` (for $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup of $Pl$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ and $z$ a $p^v$-torsion class of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the geometric function field of $X_H$, any level-$v$ point with $\Delta$-image $\sigma z - z$ has $\psi_v$-restriction congruent to the counit).
--
--   The integral-model data are a ring map $\rho_h : R_p \to Rh$ compatible with the maps to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (`hρh`) and morphisms $\iota_v : \operatorname{Spec}(\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\ v) \to O.G$ subject to: the base compatibility `hιbase`; the closed-immersion property of the induced map to the pullback of $O.g$ along $\operatorname{Spec} \rho_h$ (`hιcl`); annihilation by $p^v$, i.e. $\iota_v$ followed by the $p^v$-multiplication of $O.L$ is the unit section over its base (`hιp`); compatibility with the point parametrisation $O.\mathrm{pts} \circ \Delta$ (`hιpts`); multiplicativity on $B$-points for every $Rh$-algebra $B$ (`hιmul`); compatibility with the transitions (`hιt`); Hecke compatibility in the form `hιhecke` (a system $\varphi$ as above which in addition intertwines $\iota_v$ with $O.\mathrm{hecke}\ S\ g$); and `hιfin`, which asserts that the canonical map $j_v$ from $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\ v)$ into the double pullback cutting out the $p^v$-kernel over $\operatorname{Spec} Rh$ is both an open and a closed immersion and that its image contains every point lying over the closed point of $Rh$.
--
--   The special-fibre data are a morphism $\sigma_p : \operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb{Z}/p) \to$ `base p` factoring the reduction of $Rh$ (`hσp`), morphisms $\iota p_v : \operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb{Z}/p \otimes_{Rh} \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\ v) \to O.G \times_{\mathrm{base}} \operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb{Z}/p)$ with the two projection identities `hιp₁` and `hιp₂`, and a pair $q_0, q_1$ of morphisms over $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb{Z}/p)$ from the base change of $O.g$ along $\sigma_p$ to the base change of $\Lambda.f$ along $\sigma_p$. These are subject to: the factorisation `hfac` of the residue point of $Pl$ through $\sigma_p$ and $\Lambda.\sigma_A$; the homomorphy `hqmul` of each $q_i$ for the base-changed group laws $(O.L)_{\sigma_p}$ and $(\Lambda.L)_{\sigma_p}$ on $T$-points; and the base-change compatibility `hqbc` of each $q_i$ with the given $O.\mathrm{abqFibre}\ i$ over the residue field of $Pl$. Further, `hjB` requires the base change $1 \otimes \psi_v$ to be injective for every $v$.
--
--   Finally, a $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{A}$ over `baseRing p` of height $h\Lambda$ with $hB = 2h\Lambda$ is given, together with an algebra map `baseRing p` $\to \mathbb{Z}/p$ realising $\sigma_p$ (`hσp'`), and levelwise morphisms $\iota'_v : \operatorname{Spec}((\mathcal{A} \otimes \mathbb{Z}/p).\mathrm{level}\ v) \to \Lambda.X \times_{\mathrm{base}} \operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb{Z}/p)$ satisfying the base identity `hι'base`, the closed-immersion property `hι'cl`, the hypothesis `hι'p` that the induced map onto the $p^v$-kernel of $(\Lambda.L)_{\sigma_p}$ is an isomorphism, multiplicativity `hι'mul` and transition compatibility `hι't`.
--
--   In this situation, let $v$ be a natural number and let $K$ be a commutative ring which is a Hopf algebra over $Rh$ and free as an $Rh$-module, together with a surjective bialgebra map $qK : \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\ v \to K$ whose kernel is the ideal generated by the image under $\psi_v$ of the augmentation ideal (the kernel of the counit algebra map) of $\mathcal{B}.\mathrm{level}\ v$ (`hkerK`).
--
--   The conclusion is that for each $i \in \{0,1\}$ the composite
--   $$\operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb{Z}/p \otimes_{Rh} K) \xrightarrow{\operatorname{Spec}(1 \otimes qK)} \operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb{Z}/p \otimes_{Rh} \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\ v) \xrightarrow{\iota p_v} O.G \times \operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb{Z}/p) \xrightarrow{q_i} \Lambda.X \times \operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb{Z}/p)$$
--   is equal to its own structure morphism to $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb{Z}/p)$, obtained by following it with $\mathrm{pullback.snd}\ \Lambda.f\ \sigma_p$, followed by the unit section $((\Lambda.L.\mathrm{baseChange}\ \sigma_p).\mathrm{one}\ (\mathbf{1}_{\operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb{Z}/p)})).1$; that is, the composite is the unit section of the base-changed group law over its base.
--
--   This is the step showing that the Raynaud-type Hopf quotient $K$ of $\mathcal{G}$ at level $v$ by the toric subgroup $\mathcal{B}$ — the multiplicative-type part of the $p^v$-kernel of the Néron object $O.G$ at $p$ — is annihilated by both descended abelian-quotient morphisms $q_i$ on the special fibre. It is used in the construction of the comparison bialgebra maps between the level-$v$ torsion of the model at $p$ and the level datum $\Lambda$, within the analysis of the semistable reduction of $J_H$ at a prime exactly dividing the level.
